Interface addresses|ip -br address|Shows concise interface states and assigned addresses.|Confirm the source interface and address before tracing a path.|UP is link state; address suffixes show prefix length. An address alone does not prove a usable route.|eth0 UP 192.0.2.20/24|Read-only Linux command; example values are documentation addresses.
Route selection|ip route get 192.0.2.10|Asks the kernel which route it would use for one destination.|Check next hop, output interface and selected source address.|via is next hop, dev is interface and src is chosen local source.|192.0.2.10 via 192.0.2.1 dev eth0 src 192.0.2.20|Read-only; substitute the actual destination and consider policy routing.
Neighbor state|ip neigh show|Displays the kernel neighbor table.|Find failed local next-hop resolution.|REACHABLE is recently confirmed; STALE can be normal; FAILED indicates unsuccessful resolution.|192.0.2.1 dev eth0 lladdr 00:11:22:33:44:55 REACHABLE|Read-only; use ip -6 neigh for IPv6-specific inspection.
DNS answer|dig example.com A|Queries DNS for IPv4 address records.|Inspect response code, returned records and TTLs.|status NXDOMAIN means the name does not exist in that response; ANSWER records include remaining TTL.|example.com. 300 IN A 192.0.2.10|Read-only network query; DNS results are illustrative.
Specific resolver|dig @192.0.2.53 example.com A|Directs a DNS query to a chosen resolver.|Compare two resolvers during inconsistent resolution.|The SERVER field confirms the queried resolver; compare status, answer and TTL, not only the final address.|SERVER: 192.0.2.53#53|Replace the documentation address with an authorized resolver; a timeout does not prove the record is absent.
DNS authority chain|dig +trace example.com|Walks DNS delegation from root toward authoritative servers.|Locate a delegation or authority discrepancy.|Each referral identifies the next authoritative servers. Failure can also reflect blocked outbound DNS from the test host.|NS referrals followed by an authoritative answer|Generates multiple DNS queries; does not test every application's resolver cache.
HTTP response|curl -I --max-time 10 https://example.com|Requests HTTP headers using HEAD with a total time limit.|Inspect status, redirects and cache headers without downloading the body.|The status line identifies response class; Location identifies redirect destination. Some services treat HEAD differently from GET.|HTTP/2 200|Read-only request but still reaches the service; use a known safe endpoint.
HTTP timing|curl -sS -o /dev/null --max-time 10 -w 'dns=%{time_namelookup} connect=%{time_connect} tls=%{time_appconnect} total=%{time_total}\n' https://example.com|Measures cumulative milestones for one HTTP request.|Distinguish name resolution, connection, TLS and total duration.|Times are cumulative seconds from request start; subtract adjacent milestones for stage estimates.|dns=0.010 connect=0.030 tls=0.070 total=0.120|Use an authorized read endpoint; one sample is not a latency distribution.
TLS verification|openssl s_client -connect example.com:443 -servername example.com -verify_return_error -verify_hostname example.com|Connects with TLS, supplies SNI and validates certificate identity.|Inspect chain, negotiated protocol and verification errors.|Check verification result and hostname; a completed encrypted handshake without verification is insufficient.|Verification: OK|Uses the system trust configuration; never interpret disabling verification as a production fix.
TCP socket detail|ss -ti state established|Shows established TCP connections with internal transport information.|Investigate retransmission, congestion and round-trip behavior.|rtt estimates round-trip time; retrans fields and congestion information provide transport clues, not application health.|ESTAB ... rtt:12.1/2.0 ...|Read-only; field availability depends on kernel and iproute2 version.
Bounded packet capture|tcpdump -ni eth0 -c 50 'host 192.0.2.10 and tcp port 443'|Captures up to fifty matching packets on one interface.|Check whether SYNs, replies and resets traverse the observed point.|S is SYN, S. is SYN-ACK, R is reset; compare direction and timing.|IP client.port > server.443: Flags [S]|Requires capture privileges; use a scoped filter and protect captured metadata or payloads.
Path probe|traceroute -n -m 15 example.com|Probes a path with increasing hop limits, bounded to fifteen hops.|Locate routing changes and broad failure boundaries.|Asterisks mean no probe reply, not necessarily forwarded traffic loss. Routers may suppress or deprioritize replies.|1 192.0.2.1 1.2 ms ...|Generates diagnostic traffic; confirm tool availability and authorized target.
